WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.269 --> 00:00:05.410
If you grew up with the 1990 miniseries adaptation
of Stephen King's It, brace yourself: the

00:00:05.410 --> 00:00:10.450
2017 film version pulls all the gore and insanity
straight from the pages of Stephen King's

00:00:10.450 --> 00:00:12.740
blood-soaked 1986 novel.

00:00:12.740 --> 00:00:17.260
The movie follows the seven kids in the Losers'
Club as they try to thwart an evil, clown-shaped

00:00:17.260 --> 00:00:19.650
nightmare in the small town of Derry, Maine.

00:00:19.650 --> 00:00:23.220
Let's take a trip down into the sewers to
look at all the Easter eggs you missed in

00:00:23.220 --> 00:00:24.220
It.

00:00:24.220 --> 00:00:25.680
Warning: Some spoilers ahead.

00:00:25.680 --> 00:00:26.990
Also, clowns.

00:00:26.990 --> 00:00:28.160
Lots of clowns.

00:00:29.990 --> 00:00:31.360
The Pennywise doll

00:00:31.369 --> 00:00:35.780
During the Losers' Club's first visit to the
run-down house at 29 Neibolt Street, Bill,

00:00:35.780 --> 00:00:40.030
Eddie, and Richie get separated, and Richie
finds himself locked in literally the worst

00:00:40.030 --> 00:00:42.660
place ever—a room full of clown dolls.

00:00:42.660 --> 00:00:46.290
But look closely, because they aren't just
any terrifying clown dolls.

00:00:46.290 --> 00:00:51.470
Just to the left of the spot where Pennywise
pops out stands a figure who may look familiar:

00:00:51.470 --> 00:00:55.719
Pennywise from the 1990 miniseries, as portrayed
by Tim Curry.

00:00:55.719 --> 00:00:56.880
Werewolf hands

00:00:56.880 --> 00:01:01.329
In both the book and the miniseries, Richie's
main fear is werewolves, a fear he gets after

00:01:01.329 --> 00:01:04.180
watching I Was A Teenage Werewolf at the local
theater.

00:01:04.180 --> 00:01:08.969
So naturally, that's the shape the evil fear-sucking
clown usually takes when it appears for him.

00:01:08.969 --> 00:01:13.459
And while there aren't any werewolves howling
at the Derry moon in the 2017 It, audiences

00:01:13.459 --> 00:01:17.920
do get a quick glimpse of that lupophobia
during the encounter at 29 Neibolt Street.

00:01:17.920 --> 00:01:21.509
While the monster advances on Eddie, Bill,
Richie, and Beverly, you can see werewolf

00:01:21.509 --> 00:01:23.579
claws start to rip through its clown gloves.

00:01:23.579 --> 00:01:27.030
It's just a quick moment, but it shows the
filmmakers were thinking of the fans even

00:01:27.030 --> 00:01:30.740
as they were deciding to pretty much forget
about CG'ing a big goofy werewolf.

00:01:34.660 --> 00:01:35.659
Tracker Brothers

00:01:35.659 --> 00:01:39.920
From Eddie's Airwolf t-shirt to the bully
Belch Huggins' Anthrax tee, the kids' outfits

00:01:39.920 --> 00:01:43.170
are all about the sweet nostalgia of childhood
in the late '80s.

00:01:43.170 --> 00:01:45.859
Which is why one shirt in particular stands
out.

00:01:45.859 --> 00:01:50.060
When Eddie, Richie, Stan, and Bill go looking
for Georgie in the drainage pipe in the Barrens

00:01:50.060 --> 00:01:53.859
early in the movie, Bill's wearing a green
shirt with a nondescript round logo in the

00:01:53.859 --> 00:01:54.859
center.

00:01:54.859 --> 00:01:58.600
If you look closely, though, you can see that
the logo says Tracker Brothers.

00:01:58.600 --> 00:02:02.240
In the novel, Tracker Brothers is a company
in Derry, and their factory is where grown-up

00:02:02.240 --> 00:02:05.689
Eddie first meets Pennywise when he gets back
to Derry.

00:02:05.689 --> 00:02:06.749
Christine

00:02:06.749 --> 00:02:10.670
Bill isn't the only It character who boasts
a cottony Easter egg in this movie—one of

00:02:10.670 --> 00:02:14.690
Eddie's shirts also hearkens back to King's
other works, with a design of an angry car

00:02:14.690 --> 00:02:16.910
with what appears to be a chrome V on the
front.

00:02:16.910 --> 00:02:21.670
If that sounds familiar, you've probably read
Christine, King's 1983 novel about a killer

00:02:21.670 --> 00:02:23.920
car—or seen the film adaptation.

00:02:23.920 --> 00:02:27.510
Interestingly enough, Christine also makes
an appearance in the It novel—or at least,

00:02:27.510 --> 00:02:31.790
a red and white 1958 Plymouth Fury being driven
by a corpse shows up.

00:02:31.790 --> 00:02:34.150
But what else could that be?

00:02:34.150 --> 00:02:35.950
Freese's Department Store

00:02:35.950 --> 00:02:38.250
Was every Easter egg in this movie printed
on a t-shirt?

00:02:38.250 --> 00:02:41.670
We also see
Richie wearing a shirt proudly advertising

00:02:41.670 --> 00:02:42.670
Freese's.

00:02:42.670 --> 00:02:46.560
You've probably never shopped there, and for
good reason—Freese's was a departme nt store

00:02:46.560 --> 00:02:51.040
in Bangor, Maine that operated from 1892 to
1985.

00:02:51.040 --> 00:02:55.370
So why would Richie wear a shirt from a defunct
store that operated in a completely different

00:02:55.370 --> 00:02:56.370
town?

00:02:56.370 --> 00:02:58.810
Well, Bangor just happens to be Stephen King's
longtime home.

00:02:58.810 --> 00:03:00.290
A nod to the creator of It?

00:03:00.290 --> 00:03:03.370
Yeah, we can definitely believe that.

00:03:03.370 --> 00:03:04.370
Silver

00:03:04.370 --> 00:03:08.160
The movie doesn't pay any special attention
to the kids' bikes, and for the most part,

00:03:08.160 --> 00:03:09.460
the audience won't either.

00:03:09.460 --> 00:03:13.460
But fans of the story may have noticed one
bike in particular that plays a big part in

00:03:13.460 --> 00:03:17.970
the original novel: Bill's silver Schwinn,
which happens to have the word "Silver" printed

00:03:17.970 --> 00:03:19.040
on the frame.

00:03:19.040 --> 00:03:23.680
In the book, that bike actually saves two
lives—once in the '50s, when Bill uses it

00:03:23.680 --> 00:03:27.740
to save Eddie from an asthma attack, and once
in the '80s, when grown-up Bill drives down

00:03:27.740 --> 00:03:31.740
the middle of traffic on a busy street to
jolt his wife out of catalepsy.

00:03:31.740 --> 00:03:35.070
Although none of that happened in the movie,
the small detail of putting a name on Bill's

00:03:35.070 --> 00:03:38.490
beloved bike probably wasn't lost on fans.

00:03:38.490 --> 00:03:39.850
The Bradley Gang

00:03:39.850 --> 00:03:43.740
The filmmakers stuck a ton of tiny details
into the background of It, but one of the

00:03:43.740 --> 00:03:47.810
best was the mural of the Bradley Gang shootout—you
get a few glimpses of it on the brick wall

00:03:47.810 --> 00:03:51.280
when Mike Hanlon hallucinates the burning
hands reaching through the door.

00:03:51.280 --> 00:03:54.990
In the book, the Bradley Gang was a group
of outlaws who were killed by the Derry townsfolk

00:03:54.990 --> 00:04:00.100
in 1929, which was the last time Pennywise
was awake before the Losers' Club confronts

00:04:00.100 --> 00:04:01.100
him.

00:04:01.100 --> 00:04:04.650
It's an excellent nod to the source material
for anyone paying attention, although most

00:04:04.650 --> 00:04:10.100
people were probably pretty distracted by
the charred hands clawing at the door frame.

00:04:10.100 --> 00:04:11.100
Deadlights

00:04:11.100 --> 00:04:14.709
In the novel, Pennywise is an ancient cosmic
being that came into our universe because

00:04:14.709 --> 00:04:18.870
humans have good imaginations, and all that
imagination and fear makes its food taste

00:04:18.870 --> 00:04:19.870
good.

00:04:19.870 --> 00:04:23.409
He's not really a clown, but clowns are scary
and good at luring children, so he kind of

00:04:23.409 --> 00:04:24.409
sticks with it.

00:04:24.409 --> 00:04:28.180
What he really is is something called deadlights,
which are energy thingies that usually look

00:04:28.180 --> 00:04:30.249
like undulating orange orbs.

00:04:30.249 --> 00:04:32.340
This guy saw them, and he went crazy.

00:04:32.340 --> 00:04:35.340
The movie doesn't mention the deadlights at
all, because, well...

00:04:35.340 --> 00:04:38.319
"I've never said that name out loud before,
it sounds stupid.

00:04:38.319 --> 00:04:40.849
Call them whatever you want."

00:04:40.849 --> 00:04:43.249
But there is one quick glimpse of the deadlights.

00:04:43.249 --> 00:04:47.060
After Pennywise kidnaps Beverly and takes
her to his sewer lair, Beverly looks down

00:04:47.060 --> 00:04:50.310
his throat to see three orange lights swirling
around each other.

00:04:50.310 --> 00:04:53.270
A moment later, she's comatose and floating
in the air.

00:04:53.270 --> 00:04:57.949
It's a giant WTF moment for anyone new to
the story, but diehard fans gave a silent

00:04:57.949 --> 00:04:58.949
cheer.

00:04:58.949 --> 00:05:02.500
Whether or not the cosmic side of Pennywise
is explored more deeply in part two is uncertain

00:05:02.500 --> 00:05:06.889
as of this video, but the quick cameo of Pennywise's
true form in the first part certainly make

00:05:06.889 --> 00:05:08.309
for a satisfying Easter egg.

00:05:08.309 --> 00:05:09.309
Thanks for watching!

00:05:09.309 --> 00:05:11.559
Click the Looper icon to subscribe to our
YouTube channel.

00:05:11.559 --> 00:05:14.069
Plus check out all this cool stuff we know
you'll love, too!

